[38;5;231m⏺[39m Good — no concrete second form yet, so I'll keep this minimal and avoid building a rules framework nobody asked for.

  [1mThe fork:[0m how much vocabulary the shared validator understands. This is the interface every future form will be
  written against, so it's the expensive thing to change later — the rest (file layout, error formatting) is cheap to
  revise.

  - [1mRequired-only[0m — just lift today's check into a shared module that takes a field list. Buys reuse with near-zero new
    concepts; costs a change to the interface the first time a form needs "min 8 characters". Rules out nothing
    permanently, but the second form likely forces a revisit.
  - [1mRequired + a small fixed set[0m ([38;5;153mrequired[39m, [38;5;153mminLength[39m, [38;5;153mpattern[39m, maybe [38;5;153memail[39m) — declarative rules per field. Buys a
    realistic second form working with no interface change; costs a bit of speculative code, and every [3mnew[0m rule type
    means editing the shared module.
  - [1mPredicate functions[0m — a rule is just [38;5;153m(value) => errorMessage | null[39m, with [38;5;153mrequired[39m/[38;5;153mminLength[39m shipped as built-in
    helpers. Buys open extensibility (a form adds a one-off rule locally, never touching the shared module); costs one
    small abstraction to learn up front.

  [1mMy recommendation: predicate functions.[0m With no known second form, guessing the right fixed rule set is exactly the
  guess we'll get wrong, and the predicate shape makes a wrong guess cheap — an unanticipated rule lives in the form
  that needs it instead of forcing a change to shared code. It's also barely more code than the fixed set.
